== Key 10 diagnosis (bringup firmware) ==
cmd i: PASS MCP ACK 0x27, idle raw 0x7F7F (no stuck inputs).
cmd r: 7F7F x10.
cmd d: 40/40 ACK at 0..20000 us idle gaps, SDA=1 SCL=1 -> bus healthy (not the 2026-10-18 contact fault).
cmd M #1 (operator unsure of instructions): only GPA3 (position 9) DOWN/UP x4. No GPA2, no GPA1.
cmd M #2 (operator pressed only the broken key x3, slowly): no events at all.
-> key 10 press never pulls any MCP input LOW = open circuit: switch, GPA2 wire, or its GND leg.
   2026-10-18 log: GPA2 worked -> new mechanical fault since then.
Next: jumper GPA2 -> GND during cmd M (isolates MCP vs key side), then continuity checks.
cmd M #3 (no events; operator action unclear).
cmd M #4: GPA2 (key 10) DOWN/UP x3 clean (3277, 5923, 9307 ms) among other keys.
-> key 10 is INTERMITTENT, not dead: dead in M #2/#3, fine in M #4. Mechanical: suspect
   cracked solder joint on key 10 legs or GPA2 header pin (same module header as the
   2026-10-18 I2C contact fault). Action: reflow key 10 joints + GPA2 header pin.
   Also seen: GPA4 (key 5) chatter DOWN/UP/DOWN within 1 ms at release; 25 ms debounce covers it.
Production firmware restored after the session.

== Keys 10 + 12 intermittent (operator: multimeter "looks fine") ==
Test protocol bug found: monitor started only after Claude's reply -> presses before the window.
Empty runs M #2/#3/A/B are therefore inconclusive. Fixed: monitor started in background first.
cmd M x2 (60 s, background): GPB0 (key 1, control) DOWN/UP x4 at 5.8-8.9 s; then ~21 s with
   no events although operator was asked to press key 10 x10 and key 12 x10.
-> keys 10 (GPA2) + 12 (GPA0) open at that time; bus healthy (cmd d 40/40 before).
Suspect: shared key GND chain broken/cracked near its end (keys 10-12), opens under flex;
   alternative: MCP header pins GPA0/GPA2. Next: continuity key 10/12 leg 2 -> MCP GND while
   pressing/flexing; jumper key 10 leg 2 straight to GND.

